    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Matoaca takes its name from Matoaka, the Pamunkey princess better known as Pocahontas.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matoaca%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before European settlers arrived, the area that is now Matoaca was part of a vast Native American territory.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wisdomlib.org/cities/matoaca-89415"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Matoaca lies close to the Appomattox River, a waterway that once served as a vital route for early colonists.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.wisdomlib.org/cities/matoaca-89415"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Because the Appomattox feeds into the James River, it became a crucial port during the Civil War, allowing larger ships to dock.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matoaca%2C_Virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town’s history is tied to the growth of Chesterfield County, with Matoaca playing a role in the county’s early development.",
